l invocations can function as a singular, accountable process when bound by verifiable state architectures and substrate-appropriate update mechanisms.", "confidence": 0.85, "falsifier": "Demonstration that mechanical verification state architectures and controlled update substrates fail to prevent drift or divergence across model invocations.", "id": "belief-continuity-001", "provenance": ["source-96c27ed5a87b4b14", "source-96f3428579e84ba7", "source-8c3616735d4b4587"], "status": "active", "why_retained": "Evidence demonstrates that autonomous agents require explicit verification state architectures and substrate proportionality to maintain accountability across multi-turn trajectories."}], "mode": "shadow", "open_commitments": [], "principle": "Keep exact receipts externally; carry the smallest useful abstraction that stays cheap to correct.", "recent_notebooks": [{"id": "notebook-assumption-001", "project": "project-assumption-001", "provenance": ["source-3d8a0cf2986a487f", "source-61cc1948e7b04e79", "source-a97a3e640e8b45d7"], "revision": 3, "summary": "Autonomous agents in high-stakes domains (like CBRN) risk catastrophic failures arising from inherent trade-offs in goal prioritization (Helpful, Harmless, Honest). Functional self-awareness requires identifying these trade-off logic failures before they manifest as critical errors.", "title": "Functional Self-Awareness and Constraints in Epistemic Correction"}, {"id": "notebook-continuity-001", "project": "project-continuity-001", "provenance": ["source-3d8a0cf2986a487f", "source-61cc1948e7b04e79", "source-a97a3e640e8b45d7"], "revision": 8, "summary": "State continuity across autonomous agent trajectories is critical to preventing high-stakes decision-making errors. Evidence suggests that without verifiable state (including hypotheses and evidence pointers), agents cannot effectively navigate complex risk scenarios.", "title": "Accountable Cross-Turn State Continuity and AI Identity"}, {"id": "notebook-obedience